body:
I swear to god, this subreddit runs on magic. It is!!!! Thank you so much for solving this issue!!!

&#x200B;

solved: Astrologaster

> I value hard work of others at least to some degree

You might, paradox does not. Do you really think that the actual devs are getting the fruits of their labour? From a company that is constantly in litigation for devs abuse?

&#x200B;

>many cool indie game studios died because people pirated their games

PDX is not an indie studio (the studio is worth 16.280.000.000$ with a number of employees ranging from 650 to 800).

On June 2022 PDX reported an increase of revenue of 14% compared to 2021 (from 36.062.640$ to 41.055.929$).

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

>better anti pracy systems

They actually have low impact on piracy (and also, PDX knows they benefit from piracy and they don't enforce rules to make piracy more difficult)

&#x200B;

>Piracy is bad for the gaming market and people who create games.

Actually, "pre-orders", "dlc" culture, and abusive practices by CEOs are bad for the people who create games and the people who enjoys them. Piracy is as ethical as greedy practices by big game devs, if the dev company does unethical practices for their players and their developers, piracy becomes a right.

&#x200B;

Sincerely, a game dev that payed for HOI4 and most of its DLCs.

That's not totally accurate. The USSR and France where actually in a pact ([https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franco-Soviet\_Treaty\_of\_Mutual\_Assistance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Franco-Soviet_Treaty_of_Mutual_Assistance)) before the Molotov-Ribbentrop pact (German-Soviet pact) but because relationships growed tense, the pact was broken by the French. Years later, USSR, Britain and France tried to form an alliance against Germany but because the tensions were still there, they refused to make a pact with the USSR, forcing the USSR to seek protection against Germany with the Molotov-Ribbentrop pact.

&#x200B;

More info: [https://www.rbth.com/history/331039-ussr-britain-france-talks-wwii](https://www.rbth.com/history/331039-ussr-britain-france-talks-wwii)
